WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.170
These days, we hear a lot about data.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.510
We know that our increasingly connected, and
digital world, is producing an abundance of

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.100
data.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.100
Data alone, might not be that exciting.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.490
But what it can enable, and the answers it
can provide, are important.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.650
Data is now vital to our organizations.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.169
To our society, and ultimately to each of
us.

00:00:29.169 --> 00:00:36.280
No matter who you are, you're either producing
it, consuming it, or leveraging it.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.890
To realize the clear benefits of data, we
need good quality data.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:45.280
We need data that is accurate, managed, and
properly maintained.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.440
We need to know where data is, who owns it,
whether it's kept current, and how to store

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.960
it properly for later use.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.860
We also need the tools, and skills to work
with this data.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.510
If we do these things right, the value of
all this data will increase, and be much more

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.760
useful in the years ahead.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:09.439
It's gonna help us solve some of the most
intractable challenges we face.

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:14.600
When we handle sensitive data, what are the
regulatory and legal requirements?

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:21.390
Using data to inform our organizational decision-making
is called data management or information management.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:25.670
To some degree, all organizations take advantage
of data management.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:30.950
However, we must ask ourselves if we understand
and manage our data correctly, and if we are

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.229
getting the most value from it.

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:38.630
Every organization falls somewhere along a
continuum where weak data management is on

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.030
one end and high-performance data management
is on the other.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.500
Where your data management performance falls
is in how well you govern the management of

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.980
data.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:52.600
In trying to achieve high performance in managing
our organizational data, we must ask whether

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:58.200
there are agreed policies and processes, accountabilities,
formal decision structures, and enforcement

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.470
rules in that management.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:05.159
The implementation of these types of qualities
is called data governance.

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:09.950
At a high level we'll define data governance
as data that is managed well.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.530
This definition also helps us to clarify that
data management and data governance are not

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.730
the same thing.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.420
&nbsp;
Managing data is concerned with the use of

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.540
data to make good business decisions and ultimately
run organizations.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.470
Data governance is concerned with the degree
to which we use disciplined behavior across

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.340
our entire organization in how we manage that
data.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.030
It's an important distinction.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.230
So, what's the bottom line?

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:42.470
Most organizations manage data, but far fewer
govern those management techniques well.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.560
What are the main principles of data governance.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.230
By principles, we mean the foundational concepts
of data governance.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:54.209
They are transparency, accountability and
standardization.

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:57.560
&nbsp;
Let's begin with the principle of transparency.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:02.990
As the word suggests, in transparency, everyone
within an organization and those who interact

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:07.360
with the organization from the outside should
be able to clearly understand and be advised

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.810
in a timely manner of the processes and impacts
of data governance.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:17.060
Transparency is essential to avoid surprises,
to gain buy-in from all those impacted people

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.030
and to build trust in the organization.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:24.090
Transparency should include being clear on
why things are being done and the value of

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.050
those actions.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:30.270
Data governance is a positive topic and that
means we want to be open and clear about it

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.530
throughout the life cycle of data management.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:38.030
As an example, an organization should have
an information security policy that clearly

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:43.330
states what kind of data must never be store
or displayed on a public website.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:47.780
This could include data on employees' birth
dates or home addresses.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.849
&nbsp;
The second principle is accountability which

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:54.230
encompasses the responsibilities of anyone
who has a role to play regarding data in an

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.120
organization.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:01.070
If a person has a role to play in data governance,
it should be fully described, understood and

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:04.340
agreed upon by all involved parties.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.290
Within a mature data governance environment,
we will hold people accountable for taking

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:11.460
specific actions at specific times.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.849
As an example, if your role requires you to
disclose what stocks you hold and to enter

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:20.459
that data by a certain date, not adhering
to this requirement could result in termination

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.349
of your employment.

00:04:22.349 --> 00:04:25.360
&nbsp;
The third principle is standardization.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.419
With standardization of data, we are concerned
with such things as how data is labeled, described

00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:32.410
and categorized.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:38.210
An example of data standardization is how
we might handle designations in an organization.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:43.300
When we capture California, is our standard
to use CA or to spell out the entire state

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.630
name.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.560
This can be a really big deal, why?

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.590
For one thing, if you wanted to do a search
for all data that includes information in

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:56.680
California, a lack of a standard will result
in incomplete results.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:02.630
That is, if you search for CA only, you may
not get the results for the full word California.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:07.140
Done right, standardization solves many challenges.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:12.240
Standardization improves quality of data,
makes it more usable across systems and, subsequently,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.300
makes it more valuable.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:18.900
What all these core data governance principles
have in common is that they support data quality

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.689
which is a central goal of data governance.

